-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- the axiomatic form's outcomes
-- The axiomatic form computes outcomes without the machine: a thread alone, with each value it
-- reads taken from a finite domain, gives its events and its result; a choice of such a run
-- per thread gives the events of a candidate; every rf (each reader to the initial memory or to
-- a writer of its value at its address) and every order of the writers of each address gives
-- the witnesses;
-- the candidates that are TSO-consistent give the outcomes.
-- (The domain must contain every value a read can return: the initial values and the values
-- written. For a program writing constants that is a finite list the caller gives.)
tso-over-w : {R : Type} → List Word → (Word → R → R) → R → R
tso-over-w nil f acc = acc
tso-over-w (cons x r) f acc = tso-over-w r f (f x acc)
